# Which LLM provider the orchestrator (and translator, substitution
# reviewer) talks to. Default is "sealion" if unset. See racik/bedrock.py
# for the full explanation of each path.
# RACIK_LLM_PROVIDER=sealion

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- sealion
# Hosted api.sea-lion.ai. Simplest option, rate-limited, off your own infra.
# Without this set, Racik answers with the rule-based fallback planner —
# same real tools and numbers, no model in the loop.
SEALION_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- bedrock
# SEA-LION imported into AWS Bedrock via Custom Model Import. Uncomment
# RACIK_LLM_PROVIDER=bedrock (or just set BEDROCK_MODEL_ARN — the factory
# auto-detects the provider from it) once you have an import job's ARN.
#
# Getting BEDROCK_MODEL_ARN requires the import job to already exist — that
# happens in the AWS console or CLI (upload SEA-LION's HF weights to S3,
# run a Custom Model Import job), not from this file. This only points
# Racik at an import that's already done.
#
# RACIK_LLM_PROVIDER=bedrock
# BEDROCK_MODEL_ARN=arn:aws:bedrock:us-east-1:123456789012:imported-model/abc123
# AWS_REGION=us-east-1
#
# AWS credentials themselves are NOT set here — boto3 reads them from its
# own standard chain: AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID + AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY (+
# AWS_SESSION_TOKEN for temporary/SSO creds) as environment variables, or
# an AWS_PROFILE pointing at a profile in ~/.aws/credentials / ~/.aws/config
# (e.g. one set up by `aws sso login`). Whichever your team's AWS access
# actually uses — set that, not a copy of it in this file.
#
# Regions with Custom Model Import: us-east-1, us-east-2, us-west-2,
# eu-central-1 only. No ap-southeast-1.
#
# Before relying on this for the live demo: run verify_deployment() (see
# racik/bedrock.py) — AWS does not guarantee tool-calling works for this
# model architecture on Bedrock, and the whole orchestrator depends on it.

# ------------------------------------------------------------ bedrock_native
# Any stock Bedrock catalog model (not SEA-LION) via the Converse API —
# the path this team actually uses on the hackathon's AWS Innovation
# Sandbox account. Set RACIK_LLM_PROVIDER=bedrock_native (or just
# BEDROCK_NATIVE_MODEL_ID — the factory auto-detects it) plus the model id
# and region.
#
# RACIK_LLM_PROVIDER=bedrock_native
# BEDROCK_NATIVE_MODEL_ID=amazon.nova-lite-v1:0
# AWS_REGION=us-east-1
#
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID / AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY / AWS_SESSION_TOKEN go here
# directly (unlike the bedrock/ section above) since the ISB sandbox
# issues temporary credentials this way — they expire ~12h after
# generation, re-export from the access portal when calls start failing
# with ExpiredTokenException.
#
# Pick an ACTIVE, ON_DEMAND-capable model id — check with
# `aws bedrock list-foundation-models`. Many Claude models on Bedrock are
# INFERENCE_PROFILE-only (need a profile ARN, not a bare modelId) or
# LEGACY (access-denied even with valid credentials); amazon.nova-lite-v1:0
# and amazon.nova-micro-v1:0 are reliable ON_DEMAND defaults.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- gateway
# Bedrock Access Gateway — an OpenAI-compatible HTTP shim in front of
# Bedrock, if your team is running one. No boto3, no code change.
# RACIK_LLM_PROVIDER=gateway
# SEALION_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8000/api/v1
# SEALION_MODEL=
